  "summary": "Cans, stars and crashes printed flat: mass media adopted as painting.",
  "description": "Pop art's silkscreen practice — Andy Warhol's Factory line above all — took mass-media imagery (soup cans, Marilyn stills, death-crash photos) and printed it with visible misregistration, repeating grids and flat commercial color. The silkscreen was the point: an industrial reproduction process inside fine art made originality itself the joke. Roy Lichtenstein's Benday-dot paintings, Richard Hamilton's collages and Paolozzi's print pop belong to the same logic; Warhol's variations (32 Campbells Soups, Marilyn diptychs) turned multiplication into portraiture. Its graphic legacy is everywhere: editorial flat color, brand-irony design, and the \"pop filter\" in every image editor.",
  "culturalContext": "Pop art's silkscreen made reproduction the subject: Warhol's Factory logic — originality as multiplication — reshaped art markets, design flat-color language and celebrity culture, and every \"pop filter\" and grid-print editorial quote descends from the Factory's squeegees.",
